 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSocket delivery for committed changes.** Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because it adds repeated reads and update lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid recurring provider fees and vendor coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets provide fast updates while keeping connection capacity, session management, reliable publication, replay, and reconnect/catch-up behavior within the system's design responsibility. See [the decision record](decisions/self-hosted-websocket-delivery.md).
